'''Venerupis''' (ven-ē-rö′pis), ''n.'' [NL. (Lamarck, 1818), later ''Venerupes'' (Swainson, 1840), < ''Venus'' (''Vener-''), 5, + L. ''rupes'',
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|''Venerupis exotica.''}}}}
a rock.] '''1.''' A genus of boring bivalve mollusks of the family ''Veneridæ'', as ''V. perforans'' or ''V. irus'' and ''V. exotica''.—'''2.''' [''l. c.''; pl. ''venerupes'' (-pēz).] A member of this genus; a Venus of the rock.

'''venerupite''' (ven-ē-rö′pīt), ''n.'' [< ''Venerupis'' + ''-ite''<sup>2</sup>.] A fossil Venus of the rock.

'''venery<sup>1</sup>''' (ven′ẹ-ri), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''venerie''; < ME. ''venerye'', ''venorye'', < OF. ''venerie'', F. ''vénerie'' (ML. ''venaria'', beasts of the chase, game), hunting, a hunting-train, a kennel, < ''vener'', < L. ''venari'', hunt, chase: see ''venation''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' The act or exercise of hunting; the sports of the chase; hunting.
{{smaller block|An outrydere that loved ''venerye''.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Gen. Prol. to C. T., l. 166.}}
We’ll make this hunting of the witch as famous As any other blast of ''venery''.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Sad Shepherd, ii. 2.}}
The right of pursuing and taking all beasts of chase or ''venary'' . . . was . . . held to belong to the king.
{{right|''Blackstone'', Com., II. xxvii.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
2†. Beasts of the chase; game.
{{smaller block|Bukkes and beris and other bestes wilde,<br>Of alle fair ''venorye'' that falles to metes.
{{right|''William of Palerne'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1685.}}}}
3†. A kennel for hunting-dogs.
{{smaller block|The ''venery'', where the beagles and hounds were kept.
{{right|''Urquhart'', tr. of Rabelais, i. 55. (''Davies.'')}}}}

¶
'''venery'''<sup>2</sup> (ven′e̤-ri), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. ''venerie'', < L. ''Veneria'' (sc. ''res''), sexual intercourse, fem. of ''Venerius'', of Venus, < ''Venus'' (''Vener-''), Venus, sexual intercourse: see ''venereous'', ''Venus''.] Gratification of the sexual desire.
{{smaller block|<poem>Having discoursd of sensuall gluttonie,
It followes now I speake of ''venerie'';
For these companions as inseperable
Are linckt together with sinnes ougly cable.</poem>
{{right|''Times’ Whistle'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 75.}}}}
{{smaller block|They are luxurious, incontinent, and prone to ''Venerie.''
{{right|''Burton'', Anat. of Mel., p. 201.}}}}

'''venesect''' (vē-nẹ̄-sekt′), ''v.'' [< L. ''vena'', vein, + ''secare'', cut: see ''vein'' and ''secant''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To cut or open a vein of; phlebotomize.

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To practise venesection: as, it was common to ''venesect'' for many diseases.

'''venesection''' (vē-nẹ̄-sek′shọn), ''n.'' [< L. ''vena'', vein, + ''sectio''(''n-''), a cutting: see ''section''.] Blood-letting from a vein; phlebotomy. {{smaller|The operation may be performed on any of the superficial veins; but either the median cephalic or the basilic in the bend of the elbow is usually selected for this purpose. (See cut under ''median''<sup>1</sup>.) A band is tied around the arm just above the elbow, so as to cause a turgescence of the veins below, and then the vein selected is opened with a sharp lancet. When the desired amount of blood has been taken away,}}

