{{smaller|See ''turpentine'' and ''larch''.—'''Venetian window.''' See ''window''.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' A native of Venice.—'''2†.''' [''l. c.''] ''pl.'' A particular fashion of hose or breeches reaching below the knee, originally brought from Venice: same as ''galligaskins'', 1.
{{smaller block|Item for a ell half of brod taffaty to make him a dublet and ''venytyons''. 12 Sh.
{{right|''Wardship of Rich. Fermor'' (1586).}}}}
'''3.''' A Venetian blind. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|There is not a single pane of glass in the town, badly closing ''venetians'' being the only means of shutting up the windows.
{{right|''E. Sartorius'', In the Soudan, p. 102.}}}}
'''4.''' ''pl.'' A heavy kind of tape or braid made for Venetian blinds, to hold the slats in place.—'''5.''' Same as ''domino'', 2.
{{smaller block|I then put off my sword, and put on my ''Venetian'' or domino, and entered the bal masqué. ''The Century'', XLII. 283.}}

'''Venetianed''' (vē-nē′shand), ''a.'' [< ''Venetian'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Furnished with Venetian blinds: as, a ''Venetianed'' window.
{{smaller block|The bookcase stood immediately in front of a double ''venetianed'' door.
{{right|''R. Hodgson'', Proc. Soc. Psych. Research, III. 256.}}}}

'''veneur''' (ve-nėr′), ''n.'' [< OF. ''veneor'', F. ''veneur'' (= Pr. ''venaire''), < L. ''venator'', a hunter, < ''venari'', hunt: see ''venation''<sup>1</sup>.] A person charged with the care of the chase, especially with the hounds used in the chase. There were mounted veneurs, and those of inferior class on foot.—{{smaller|'''Grand veneur,''' an officer of the French court charged with the arrangements for the king’s hunting: in later}}

