'''venew†''', '''veney†''', ''n.'' Same as ''venue''<sup>1</sup>.

'''Venezuelan''' (ven-e-zwē′lạn), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ''Venezuela'' (see def.) + ''-an.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' Of or pertaining to Venezuela, a republic of South America, on the northern coast.
{{smaller block|Guzman Blanco could not procure an audience with Lord Salisbury to protest against British seizures of ''Venezuelan'' territory at the north of the Orinoco.
{{right|''Amer. Economist'', III. 169.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Venezuelan ipecacuanha''', a climbing plant of Venezuela, ''Philibertia (Sarcostemma) glauca.''}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' An inhabitant of Venezuela.

'''venge†''' (venj), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''vengen'', < OF. (and F.) ''venger'' = Sp. ''vengar'', < L. ''vindicare'', avenge, vindicate: see ''vindicate.'' Cf. ''avenge'', ''revenge'', ''vengeance.''] '''1.''' To avenge; take vengeance in behalf of (a person).
{{smaller block|Right as they han ''venged'' hem on me, right so shal I ''venge'' me upon hem. {{right|''Chaucer'', Tale of Melibeus.}}
<poem>I am coming on
To ''venge'' me as I may. ''Shak.'', Hen. V., i. 2. 292.</poem>}}
'''2.''' To revenge; take vengeance because of (an offense).
{{smaller block|Would none but I might ''venge'' my cousin’s death!
{{right|''Shak.'', R. and J., iii. 5. 87.}}}}

'''vengeable†''' (ven′jạ-bl), ''a.'' [Early mod. E. also ''vengible''; < OF. *''vengeable'' (= Sp. ''vengable''); as ''venge'' + ''-able.''] '''1.''' Capable of being or deserving to be revenged.
{{smaller block|<poem>I sought
Upon myselfe that ''vengeable'' despight
To punish. ''Spenser'', F. Q., II. iv. 30.</poem>}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
'''2.''' Characterized by revengefulness; entertaining or displaying a desire for revenge; vengeful.
{{smaller block|<poem>In mallyce be not ''vengeable,''
As S. Mathewe doth speake.</poem>
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 92.}}
Alexander . . . dyd put to ''vengeable'' deth his dere frende Clitus.
{{right|''Sir T. Elyot,'' The Governour, ii. 6.}}}}

'''3.''' Terrible; dreadful; awful; extraordinary: a hyperbolical use.
{{smaller block|Paulus . . . was a ''vengible'' fellow in linking matters together.
{{right|''Holland,'' tr. of Camden, p. 78. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''vengeably†''' (ven′ja̤-bli), ''adv.'' Revengefully; in revenge.
{{smaller block|Charitably, lovingly, not of malice, not ''vengeably,'' not covetously.
{{right|''Latimer,'' 4th Sermon bef. Edw. VI., 1594.}}}}

'''vengeance''' (ven′jans), ''n.'' [< ME. ''vengeance, vengeaunce, venjaunce, vengeans, vengance, vengaunce,'' < OF. ''vengeance, venjance,'' F. ''vengeance'' (= Sp. ''venganza'' = It. ''vengianza''), < ''venger,'' avenge: see ''venge.''] '''1.''' Punishment inflicted in return for an injury or an offense. {{smaller|Vengeance generally implies indignation on the part of the punisher, and more or less justice in the nature of the punishment; it may also be inflicted for wrong done to others, as well as to the punisher, in which respects it is usually distinguished from revenge.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Veniaunce, veniaunce'' forȝiue be it neuere.</poem>
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xvii. 288.}}
''Vengeance'' is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord.
{{right|Rom. xii. 19.}}}}

'''2†.''' Harm, mischief, or evil generally: formerly often used as an imprecation, especially in the phrase ''what a'' (''the'') ''vengeance!''

